We are given the functional differential equation: $$
f'(x) = \frac{f(x+n) - f(x)}{n}
$$

Let’s write the equation again: $$
f'(x) = \frac{f(x+n) - f(x)}{n}
$$

Let’s suppose $ f $ is differentiable and satisfies the condition: $$
n f'(x) = f(x+n) - f(x) \quad \text{for all } x \in \mathbb{R}, \, n \in \mathbb{N}
$$

Then $ g $ satisfies: $$
g'(x) = \frac{g(x+n) + an + b - (g(x) + ax + b)}{n} = \frac{g(x+n) - g(x) + a(n - x)}{n}
$$

Therefore, the **only solutions** are: $$
\boxed{f(x) = ax + b \quad \text{for some constants } a, b \in \mathbb{R}}
$$

### ✅ Final Answer: $$
\boxed{f(x) = ax + b \text{ for some } a, b \in \mathbb{R}}
$$
